    "title": "Democrats Put Public Safety at Risk, Says Kauffman",
    "url": "https://www.repkauffman.com/News/38375/Press-Releases/Democrats-Put-Public-Safety-at-Risk--Says-Kauffman",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Democrats Put Public Safety at Risk, Says Kauffman\nJuly 23, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– Legislation addressing the sentencing of convicted murders will miss the state deadline due to House Democrat maneuvers, potentially putting killers back on the streets, said Rep. Rob Kauffman (R-Franklin).\nOn March 26, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled in Commonwealth of Pennsylvania v. Derek Lee, that automatic mandatory life without parole sentences for second-degree murder (also known as felony murder) are a violation of the state Constitution. The court gave the Legislature 120 days to make changes to the unconstitutional sentencing framework. That deadline is Friday, July 24. Without new measures in place addressing sentencing, hundreds of men and women already convicted of second-degree murder could be resentenced or released from incarceration.\nSecond-degree murder applies when an individual participates in certain dangerous felonies, including robbery, rape or arson, that result in another person’s death. Even if the individual did not personally cause the fatal injury, Pennsylvania law has long recognized that those who willingly participate in violent felonies that lead to a loss of life must be held accountable.\nCurrently in Pennsylvania, approximately 1,100 inmates are serving life sentences for second-degree murder.\n“Democrats avoided action on the bill until the day before the deadline. This inaction is now putting public safety at risk,” said Kauffman. “In addition, by amending the bill at this late stage, they have all but guaranteed the legislation will not be adopted by the deadline.”\nKauffman opposed the bill due to the amendments the Democrats added, which included provisions to allow expansive medical parole for inmates; and new sentencing guidelines that do not include any mandatory sentences, instead giving judges complete discretion.\n“We are talking about violent offenders, such as rapists and arsonists, who are responsible for the death of an individual or individuals,” said Kauffman. “Victims and survivors deserve better than dramatically reduced sentences, expansive medical parole and no minimum mandatory sentencing for these grievous crimes.”\nHouse Bill 1042\nnow goes back to the state Senate for consideration.\nEditor’s Note: A video clip of Rep. Rob Kauffman’s comments is available for viewing and distribution. To download the clip, follow the link, click the three dots in the lower right corner of the screen and select download.\nWatch the video here\n.\nRepresentative Rob Kauffman\n89th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": null
  },
